Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma
Haematology and Oncology
Painless generalized lymphadenopathy, Rapid non-contiguous extranodal spread, B symptoms (fever/night sweats/weight loss), Malignant clonal proliferation lacking Reed-Sternberg cells

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: HMO-034
Reason for Visit: Acute development of profound drenching night sweats, severe rigors, and rapid expansion of asymmetrical tonsillar and cervical swelling causing swallowing discomfort.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an adult patient experiencing a sudden, high fever with uncontrollable shivering, combined with a rapidly expanding lump in their throat causing difficulty swallowing liquids. Urgent in-person evaluation was clinically mandated to rule out acute airway compromise, severe deep neck space infection, or rapid neoplastic expansion, which could not wait until the next morning.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "I woke up completely drenched in sweat, shaking violently, and the lump on the side of my neck has grown so fast today I can barely swallow water."
History of Present Illness: The 58-year-old patient reports a 6-week insidious history of painless, firm, and mobile generalized lymphadenopathy in the neck and groin. They endorse an unexplained significant weight loss of 7 kg over this period. Tonight, they experienced the acute onset of severe drenching night sweats and recurrent high fevers. Concurrently, they noticed a rapid, painful expansion of a previously stable mass in the right neck and throat, leading to progressive odynophagia and a sensation of a partial blockage when swallowing. They report profound systemic malaise.
Functional Status: Bedbound, sitting upright propped on pillows to ease swallowing and breathing.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of sudden total airway obstruction overnight.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 115/70 | HR: 118 | Temp: 38.8 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 97 percent | Wt: 76 Kg
General: Pale, diaphoretic adult in acute distress, visibly shivering and holding their neck.
Physical Exam:
Lymphatic: Massive, asymmetrical right cervical lymphadenopathy extending rapidly into the submandibular space. Painless, firm, mobile nodes are also palpated in the left axillary and bilateral inguinal regions (non-contiguous spread).
